  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-4 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-4I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(D)={D}^{2}-4I.$ \n\nLet's calculate ${D}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(D):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & ={D}^{2}-4I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-4 & 0+0 \\\\ 0+0 & 4-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
